<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Sculpture in gold and in jade (system: mining — a mined mineral that something is finally made out of).

    THE HOLE THIS CLOSES. Buildings_Mineable.xml yields Flint, Salt, Coal, Copper, Tin, Silver, Gold, Jade,
    Uranium and Plasteel, and before this file not one of those ten was named by a single recipe ingredient
    or a single costList anywhere in content. A settlement could mine a gold vein out and the gold would lie
    on the ground until the end of the game. Two of the ten are wired here; the other eight are not, and this
    lane's report says why rather than pretending otherwise.

    WHY GOLD AND JADE, AND WHY SCULPTURE. Of the ten, these are the two whose own descriptions in
    Items_Minerals.xml already say what they are for — gold is "useless for a tool and unmatched for saying
    who you are", jade is "carved into ornaments by peoples who had nothing harder to carve it with" — and
    they are the two RimWorld itself spends on art rather than on tools (its gold and jade are stuffs, and
    what its players make out of them is sculpture). They are also the cheapest to wire honestly, because the
    consumer already exists and is real: Buildings_Art.xml ships a wooden Sculpture with a Beauty statBase,
    Needs/BeautyUtility sums that stat off the cells a citizen can see, and Need_Beauty turns it into mood.
    So a gold sculpture is a mined mineral going into something a citizen actually feels, not another product
    added to a pile nothing consumes — which is the exact defect this lane exists to stop repeating.

    It also closes the second half of a finding the stonecutting lane recorded: Sculpture had a
    Blueprint/Frame pair and nothing anywhere placed one. Building.SettlementWorksInitiative now does, and
    Building.SculptureMaterials picks which of the three a settlement raises next — the same shape
    StoneWallMaterials already uses to pick a wall's stone, and for the same reason (this port costs a
    building through costList, so one Def per material is the faithful fallback until stuff-chosen
    construction lands; see that class's own remarks).

    UNGATED BY RESEARCH, like the wooden Sculpture it parallels. Gold and jade are both worked cold, by
    peoples with no metallurgy at all — that is most of the point of them — so a researchPrerequisite would
    be this port inventing a gate RimWorld does not have and history does not support.

    THE NUMBERS.
      costList 25 is NOT a new figure: it is the count the shipped wooden Sculpture already costs, reused
      unchanged so the material is the only thing that differs between the three. A gold vein yields 20
      (Buildings_Mineable.xml), so one sculpture is a little over one vein — which is the sort of price a
      thing like this should have.

      MaxHitPoints stays at the wooden sculpture's 100 for both, deliberately: nothing in this port attacks a
      sculpture, so a per-material figure would be three numbers invented to be read by nothing.

      Beauty and WorkToBuild ARE this port's own and are NOT sourced — RimWorld expresses both as per-stuff
      factors on one Sculpture def and those factors were not checkable from this sandbox. What is sourced is
      the ordering, and it is read off content rather than asserted here: gold and jade both outshine wood,
      and jade sits above gold because Items_Minerals.xml already prices jade (12) above gold (10) on the one
      MarketValue chain that file fixes. Jade also costs the most work, which its own description asks for
      ("blunts the tools that shape it"). SettlementWorksTests pins that ordering and the wood-versus-mineral
      gap; it does not pin 60/70 or 1200/1400.

    Its own file, and it touches nothing that already exists: the two ThingDefs are new, and their
    Blueprint_/Frame_ pairs follow Buildings_BlueprintsAndFrames.xml's hand-authored pattern without that file
    changing (CLAUDE.md's "add a file rather than edit a shared one").
  -->

  <ThingDef Name="BaseMineralSculpture" Abstract="True">
    <category>Building</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Building.Building</thingClass>
    <passability>Impassable</passability>
    <fillPercent>0.4</fillPercent>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineralSculpture">
    <defName>SculptureGold</defName>
    <label>gold sculpture</label>
    <description>A figure worked in soft yellow metal. Nothing else a settlement owns says quite so plainly what it has.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>100</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>1200</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>60</Beauty>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
    <costList>
      <Gold>25</Gold>
    </costList>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineralSculpture">
    <defName>SculptureJade</defName>
    <label>jade sculpture</label>
    <description>Green stone ground down over months into a shape. It blunted every tool that touched it, and that is half of why it is looked at.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>100</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>1400</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>70</Beauty>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
    <costList>
      <Jade>25</Jade>
    </costList>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef>
    <defName>Blueprint_SculptureGold</defName>
    <label>gold sculpture (blueprint)</label>
    <category>Blueprint</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Building.Blueprint</thingClass>
    <passability>Standable</passability>
    <entityToBuild>SculptureGold</entityToBuild>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef>
    <defName>Frame_SculptureGold</defName>
    <label>gold sculpture (under construction)</label>
    <category>Frame</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Building.Frame</thingClass>
    <passability>Impassable</passability>
    <fillPercent>0.4</fillPercent>
    <entityToBuild>SculptureGold</entityToBuild>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef>
    <defName>Blueprint_SculptureJade</defName>
    <label>jade sculpture (blueprint)</label>
    <category>Blueprint</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Building.Blueprint</thingClass>
    <passability>Standable</passability>
    <entityToBuild>SculptureJade</entityToBuild>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef>
    <defName>Frame_SculptureJade</defName>
    <label>jade sculpture (under construction)</label>
    <category>Frame</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Building.Frame</thingClass>
    <passability>Impassable</passability>
    <fillPercent>0.4</fillPercent>
    <entityToBuild>SculptureJade</entityToBuild>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
